{"type":"message","id":"dd150cb9","parentId":"4f02551f","timestamp":"2026-02-16T15:50:27.321Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good! I can see paired.json contains a device that's paired - looks like the CLI client (clientId: \"cli\", clientMode: \"cli\", role: \"operator\"). This is the gateway itself paired with the system.\n\npending.json is empty {} - no pending pairing requests.\n\nNow I need to find if there are any WhatsApp-specific logs. The main logs directory only has config-audit.jsonl. There might be a separate log location for gateway or WhatsApp plugin. Let me search for .log files recursively or check if there's a different logging setup.\n\nAlso, I should check the identity files and maybe the workspace state to understand the full picture.\n\nBut the most critical finding is: in the WhatsApp credentials, \"registered\": false. This means the WhatsApp account has not been fully registered/completed with the WhatsApp servers. That's likely why messages aren't working.\n\nLet me look for any gateway logs or error logs that might explain why registration failed. Maybe there's an error log in the subagents directory or somewhere else.\n\nI should also check if there's a WhatsApp plugin configuration file that might have settings like API key or local client info.\n\nLet me search for log files in the entire .openclaw directory.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"Let me search more thoroughly for logs and check the WhatsApp plugin configuration:"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_8f95cbbd1f0042ea88d1427a","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"powershell -Command \"Get-ChildItem -Path C:\\Users\\mangs\\.openclaw -Recurse -Include *.log,*.txt | Select-Object FullName, Length, LastWriteTime | Format-Table -AutoSize\"","pty":true}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_4eaf24832eca46b4bc7f07cf","name":"read","arguments":{"file_path":"C:/Users/mangs/.openclaw/identity/device.json"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"ca